  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the equation $9{x}^{2}-18x=7.$  Write the solutions in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*\n\n$\\,{x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-18x & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides and then dividing by $9$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9+9 & =7+9 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
